**Write the STATUS file at Step 0 and update it after every Step and every jest hold; a STATUS that does not exist while your logs move is a failure of this brief.** At Step 0, before any heavy work, write `/Volumes/Laptop-DEV/!CODING/Datasec/NexusAI/1_Project_Definition/Briefs/2026-10-26_B713_STATUS.md`. **Its FIRST line is a placeholder** (`B713 STATUS (placeholder, Step 0 running)`, replaced by the real title when Step 0 completes). It holds the Step-0 block (sha256s of source and copy, the slot count with its control, the REFUSE counts with controls, pane name, `gh auth status` result in words, `core.sshCommand` path, lock-tool hash, the `brief_seat: model=...` line, target and hard-stop times written absolute), then one line per Step (1 to 9) saying not started / running / done / STOPPED with the evidence path. **The LAST LINE of the STATUS FILE is, while you work, `WORKING: <step and what is running, with a figure that moves>`, rewritten after every Step; the final marker below replaces it as the FILE's last line only when you mean it. Do not write a `READY FOR QA`, `PARTIAL:`, `STOPPED:` or `BLOCKED:` line anywhere in the file (not even as a draft or a quotation at the start of a line) until you mean it: Tuesday's watcher reads only the file's last line.** Heartbeat: every waiter and every long loop prints a figure that MOVES (a load1 reading, a loop counter `unit i of N`, elapsed seconds, a growing log size) at least every 30 s into `session-tools/b713/logs/heartbeat.txt`; a figure that does not move for 3 beats means the waiter is stuck: say so in STATUS and what you did. Never wait with `pgrep -f <pattern>` from a shell whose own argv holds the pattern; wait on a recorded pid (`kill -0`) or a done-marker file your job writes last. **RESUME at about 80 percent of your context:** write `WORKING: RESUME NEEDED at Step <n>` and a RESUME section (what is done, the next command, every path and pid, the campaign totals so far) and stop adding new work; Tuesday re